\section*{Integral \#11 (Medium)}
\[
I=\lim_{h\to0^+}\int_0^h\frac{x\cot^{-1}(\pi x)}{\sin(hx)}dx
\]
Solution Methodology:
\begin{itemize}
\item Expand the sine via its Maclaurin series:
\[
\sin(hx)=hx+\mathcal{O}\left((hx)^3\right)
\]
\item Pull out a factor of $hx$:
\[
I=\lim_{h\to0^+}\frac{1}{h}\int_0^h\frac{\cot^{-1}(\pi x)}{1+\mathcal{O}\left((hx)^2\right)}dx
\]
\item Apply the MVT:
\[
I=\lim_{h\to0^+}\frac{\cot^{-1}(\pi x_h)}{1+\mathcal{O}\left((hx_h)^2\right)},\quad0\leq x_h\leq h
\]
\item Take the limit via continuity
\end{itemize}
\[
\boxed{\;I=\pi/2\;}
\]

